=== C4 -- JUDGEMENT: does brief-criterion 3 ("no as_of => output unchanged")
          hold on a fixture where recurring detection DOES fire? -- FAIL ===
Answer, in two parts, both from commands.

(4a) On the recurring dimension the answer is YES, but VACUOUSLY, and for a
reason neither the worker nor the brief identified. I built four default-path
fixtures where detection genuinely fires and ran them in BOTH trees
(probe_test.go, `go test -count=1 -v -run TestProbe ./internal/services/
mcpsvc/ledger/` in $S/fixed and $S/mutant, outputs diffed):
  A-default-recent  (series ending 30d ago)  min=1000.00 crossing= top_up=0.00
  B-default-stale   (series ending 140d ago) min=1000.00 crossing= top_up=0.00
  C-default-future-rows                      min=1000.00 crossing= top_up=0.00
  D-default-mod-stale                        min=1000.00 crossing= top_up=0.00
  diff fixed vs mutant -> IDENTICAL   (also identical under TZ=UTC)
Detection DOES fire on fixture A -- direct instrumentation in $S/fixed
(TestProbeDiag) prints:
  DIAG DetectRecurring(old ref=maxdate) count=1
  DIAG   old rp desc="rent" freq="monthly" amt=900.00 next=2026-08-19 legs=3
  DIAG DetectRecurringAt(ref=now)        count=1  (same item)
yet accounts.Project applies no occurrence. Root cause, demonstrated:
  TestProbeH, same fixture, two calls --
    default (no as_of)      as_of=2026-08-19 minimum=1000.00 crossing=""
    explicit as_of=2026-08-19 as_of=2026-08-19 minimum=100.00 crossing="2026-09-18"
  Identical in fixed AND mutant, so PRE-EXISTING, not an R5 regression.
  internal/services/accounts/projection.go:136 writes byDay[occ] where occ
  derives from rp.NextExpected (Location = time.UTC); projection.go:117/149-150
  read byDay[day] where day derives from dayOf(asOf) with asOf = time.Now()
  (Location = time.Local). Go's map key equality on time.Time compares the
  *Location pointer, verified independently:
    $S/tz `go run .`  -> t1==t2: false, m[t2] found=false
    TZ=UTC `go run .` -> t1==t2: false (Equal true), m[t2] found=false
  => with as_of omitted, get_balance_projection can NEVER apply a recurring
  item, in any timezone. The committed pin therefore proves no-drift on a
  path where R5's recurring change is structurally unobservable. This is a
  real production defect (MCP default projection is always flat and disagrees
  with the dashboard, which uses asOf = ledger maxDate in UTC) but it lives in
  internal/services/accounts/projection.go, OUTSIDE R5's manifest -- report to
  the lead as a new task, not chargeable to R5.

(4b) On a dimension the worker did not consider, the answer is NO: the default
path's output DID change. accounts.Project:174 calls
medianInboundPairedTransfer(acct, txs), which applies NO date filter of its
own (projection.go:249-265) -- it is filtered only by whatever slice the
handler hands it, and R5 changed that slice. TestProbeG (identical file in
both trees), default path, checking with confirmed inbound paired transfers of
100 and 200 in the past and 900 dated today+10:
  FIXED : PROBEG default-path: has_ref=true reference_amount=150.00
  MUTANT: PROBEG default-path: has_ref=true reference_amount=200.00
A user-visible dollar figure ("you usually move $X") changed on the no-as_of
path. Brief criterion 3 says "output is unchanged from current behaviour ...
pin this so the default path cannot drift". It is not unchanged, and
TestGetBalanceProjectionDefaultAsOfUnchanged (accounts_test.go:546-583) cannot
catch it: its fixture contains no transfers at all and asserts has_reference
== false. The pin is vacuous in both dimensions.
The new value is arguably the more correct one, but that is a decision the
brief did not authorize silently; the criterion as written is not met and the
regression pin does not do the job it was written to do.

=== C5 -- FilterByDateRange inclusivity vs Project/BalanceAt -- FAIL ===
Same-day semantics MATCH. models/transaction.go:225 sets
endDay = end-of-day 23:59:59.999999999 and :228 tests !t.Date.After(endDay);
accounts/balance.go:63 tests txDay.After(anchorDay) && !txDay.After(atDay).
Anchor = END-of-day, as_of = inclusive. Demonstrated end-to-end (TestProbeE/F,
explicit UTC as_of):
  E-asof-boundary as_of=2026-06-15 minimum=2900.00  (06-15 row -100 counted,
                                                     06-16 row -200 excluded)
  F-anchor-day    as_of=2026-06-15 minimum=2800.00  (anchor-day row not
                                                     re-applied; 06-06 row is)

BUT R5's new FilterByDateRange call is off by one day on the DEFAULT path, and
the error is timezone-dependent. transaction.go:224-225 builds the boundary in
end.Location(); as_of on the default path is time.Now() (Local), while
transaction dates are UTC. TestProbeK in $S/fixed prints:
  PROBEK tx.Date=2026-08-20 00:00:00 +0000 UTC loc="UTC"
  PROBEK time.Now() loc="Local" offset=-04:00
  PROBEK FilterByDateRange(min, time.Now()) kept 1 of 1 tomorrow-dated rows
i.e. a row dated TOMORROW survives "as of today" west of UTC, while BalanceAt
(dayOf comparison) excludes it -- the two boundaries inside one call disagree.
It moves money on screen. TestProbeJ, future inbound transfer of 900:
  local TZ (EDT, UTC-04): today+1 -> reference_amount=200.00 (wrongly included)
                          today+2 -> reference_amount=150.00
  TZ=Asia/Tokyo (UTC+09): today+1 -> reference_amount=150.00
                          today+2 -> reference_amount=150.00
Same data, same call, different answer depending on the server's timezone.
The criterion explicitly names this risk ("dayOf truncation is used elsewhere.
An off-by-one day here changes money figures"). Missing: normalise as_of to a
day boundary in a single, consistent location (the dayOf convention already
used by balance.go:201-203 and projection.go:117) before filtering, and pin it
with a test at the today/tomorrow boundary. Explicit-as_of callers are
unaffected (time.Parse yields UTC), which is why the committed tests pass.

