=== C8 -- R3 parity test still passes; is agreement now STRUCTURAL? -- PASS,
          with a caveat ===
Command: `go test -race -count=1 -run TestDashboardAndMCPProjectionAgree -v
./internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/` -> --- PASS (0.16s).
Structural: YES for the recurring input. TestProbeI (C1 above) shows the MCP
recurring list is now element-for-element identical (description, amount,
frequency, NextExpected) to the dashboard's
insights.DetectRecurringAt(ts.Active(), asOf) at every as_of tested, including
as_of far past the ledger max, whereas the pre-R5 body diverged there
(preR5MCP=1 vs dashboard=0 at as_of=2026-12-01). The handler's extra
FilterByDateRange is idempotent with DetectRecurringAt's own
TransactionSetForRecurring truncation. Before R5 the two paths agreed only
because dashboard/handlers.go:111-114 sets asOf = maxDate, which is the same
value DetectRecurring's zero referenceDate happened to fall back to --
coincidence, exactly as the brief says.
Caveat (not a criterion, but it limits the claim): the two paths still pass
DIFFERENT slices to accounts.Project -- dashboard/handlers.go:114 passes
data.Active().Transactions untruncated, MCP passes the truncated slice. That is
unobservable at the dashboard's own asOf (= maxDate, nothing after it) but is
the mechanism behind the C4b reference_amount divergence. And per C4a the MCP
default path never applies recurring at all, so MCP-with-no-as_of and the
dashboard do NOT agree in production.

